  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194340/assets/57f5c6e193c1db7dc05024088bdcae5cef890c252eb0c06621e36174e1353a0e.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-2,-1).$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $\\frac{5}{2}.$ This occurs at the point $(5,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\frac{5}{2},$ including $\\frac{5}{2}$ but not including $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1<y\\le \\frac{5}{2},$ which can also be written as  \n\n$$\n-1<f(x)\\le \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$",
